    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d210">

                <pb facs="0344" n="332" sameAs="frus:frus1945v06/pg332"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114A/5–245: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-02T16:00:00+01:00">May 2, 1945—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 2—3:13 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2569. American Interests—Japan. Your 1222, March 26.<anchor n="37"
                        xml:id="d210-f1"/> Swiss note April 30 states Swiss Legation Tokyo reports
                    increasing difficulties in obtaining authorization visit camps because of
                    bombardments in areas where camps located.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d210-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d210-f1" n="37" xml:id="d210-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it requested the latest available information regarding
                    prisoner-of-war camps in or near Yokohama, Tokyo, Osaka, Kobe, and Nagoya
                    (711.-94114A/3–2645).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d210-f2" n="38" xml:id="d210-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 3057, June 5, 7 p.m., the Minister in Switzerland reported that Mr.
                    Gorgé, in continuing his efforts to obtain authorization to visit POW camps, had
                    addressed an urgent letter to “Minister Tojo” (711.94114A/6–545), presumably
                    Shigenori Togo who became Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs on April 9,
                    1945.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
